body:
> greenlet does not assume that when you switch greenlets, that you would want contextvars to remain in the same context

You are right, similar to `asyncio.Task`, greenlets run in their own *contexts*. Switching between `asyncio.Task` or greenlets would cause literally the PEP-567 *context* switch too.

The difference between `asyncio.Task` and greenlet is, a new `asyncio.Task` will inherit the context from where it's created:

[python/cpython: Lib/asyncio/tasks.py](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/d9ab95ff1fe81efdf70e545d536d9f6927d1ba81/Lib/asyncio/tasks.py#L134)
```python
class Task(futures._PyFuture):
    def __init__(self, coro, *, loop=None, name=None):
        ...
        self._context = contextvars.copy_context()
```

Or the C version:

[python/cpython: Modules/_asynciomodule.c](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/d9ab95ff1fe81efdf70e545d536d9f6927d1ba81/Modules/_asynciomodule.c#L2079)
```c
static int
_asyncio_Task___init___impl(TaskObj *self, PyObject *coro, PyObject *loop,
                            PyObject *name)
{
    ...
    Py_XSETREF(self->task_context, PyContext_CopyCurrent());
```

But greenlets start fresh with an empty context (`NULL`, no initial value set):

[python-greenlet/greenlet: greenlet.h](https://github.com/python-greenlet/greenlet/blob/71ecaa8188894dccdf35351614e65f07752c2414/greenlet.h#L50-L52)
```c
typedef struct _greenlet {
    ...
	PyObject* context;
} PyGreenlet;
```

Therefore we needed the extra `contextvars.copy_context()`, which is the same C implementation:

[python/cpython: Modules/_contextvarsmodule.c](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/d9ab95ff1fe81efdf70e545d536d9f6927d1ba81/Modules/_contextvarsmodule.c#L15-L20)
```c
static PyObject *
_contextvars_copy_context_impl(PyObject *module)
{
    return PyContext_CopyCurrent();
}
```

(I think new greenlets should call this by default, but looks like they decided the opposite way that greenlets are more like threads (start fresh) than tasks (inherit context). There was a similar discussion even before PEP-567 in GINO: python-gino/gino#84)

> specifically the thing happening in the PR looks like it may reduce performance?

So other than the tuple concatenation, I'd expect the performance impact to be minimal. The `PyContext_CopyCurrent()` function itself doesn't really copy - because the underlying dictionary is immutable:

[python/cpython: Python/context.c](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/cde283d16d87024f455e45c6f1b4e4f7d8905836/Python/context.c#L391-L404)
```c
PyObject *
PyContext_CopyCurrent(void)
{
    PyContext *ctx = context_get();
    if (ctx == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    return (PyObject *)context_new_from_vars(ctx->ctx_vars);
}

static PyContext *
context_new_from_vars(PyHamtObject *vars)
{
    PyContext *ctx = _context_alloc();
    if (ctx == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    Py_INCREF(vars);
    ctx->ctx_vars = vars;

    _PyObject_GC_TRACK(ctx);
    return ctx;
}
```

https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0567/#implementation

> The Context mapping is implemented using an immutable dictionary. This allows for a O(1) implementation of the copy_context() function.
